<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The entitlements a RELEASE build of Sonny is signed with (SONNY-156).

  This file is the single place release entitlements are named, the way Packaging/signing-identity
  is the single place the signing identity is named. scripts/package-app.sh reads it for release
  builds only; debug builds keep signing with SwiftPM's own generated plist, which is what lets a
  debugger attach and is correct for local development.

  WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT: com.apple.security.get-task-allow.

  SwiftPM writes that entitlement into .build/<triple>/debug/MacAgent-entitlement.plist and Apple's
  notary service rejects any submission carrying it. Measured at 7b9fec9: SwiftPM generates that
  file for debug and NOT for release, so a release build never carried the entitlement in the first
  place — but that was an accident of the toolchain rather than a decision, and nothing checked it.
  package-app.sh now reads the sealed entitlements back off the signed release bundle and refuses to
  finish if get-task-allow is present, so the absence is verified rather than assumed.

  A NOTE ON EDITING THIS FILE. It may not contain a literal double hyphen anywhere, including
  inside this comment, because XML forbids that sequence in comments. codesign's entitlements
  parser enforces it and reports "AMFIUnserializeXML: syntax error near line N", which reads like a
  problem with the entitlements rather than with the prose above them. `plutil -lint` accepts the
  file regardless, so it is not the check that catches this. Write codesign flags as "options
  runtime" rather than with their leading hyphens.

  WHY THE TWO KEYS BELOW EXIST. Release builds are signed with the hardened runtime
  (codesign, options runtime), which notarization requires. Hardened runtime also restricts access
  to resources that are unrestricted without it, and Sonny uses two of them. Adding the runtime flag
  without these keys would trade a loud notarization rejection for voice input and Finder/Word
  automation failing silently in the shipped build, which is the worse bug.

  com.apple.security.device.audio-input
      AudioCommandRecorder.swift calls AVCaptureDevice.requestAccess(for: .audio) for push-to-talk
      and the widget's mic button; PermissionReadinessService reads the same authorization status.
      Under hardened runtime that access needs this entitlement. Paired with Info.plist's
      NSMicrophoneUsageDescription, which is what makes the system prompt legible — the two are
      different mechanisms and both are required.

  com.apple.security.automation.apple-events
      FinderContextService and DocumentConverter drive Finder and Microsoft Word.

      A JUDGMENT CALL, recorded as one rather than stated as a fact. Sonny sends no Apple Events
      in-process — verified at 7b9fec9 by grepping Sources/ for NSAppleScript,
      NSAppleEventDescriptor and AEDeterminePermissionToAutomateTarget, which returns nothing.
      Every event leaves through /usr/bin/osascript spawned as a child (FinderContextService.swift:55,
      DocumentConverter.swift:98), and osascript is Apple-signed and already entitled. So the process
      actually sending the event may not be this one — while TCC attributes the automation to the
      responsible parent, which is why Info.plist carries NSAppleEventsUsageDescription naming Sonny.
      Whether the hardened-runtime check follows the sender or the responsible process cannot be
      settled without a real notarized build.

      It is included because the costs are asymmetric: granting it when it is unnecessary permits
      nothing beyond sending Apple Events and does not affect notarization, while omitting it when it
      is needed breaks Finder context and DOCX conversion in the first build a stranger runs. The
      founder's first notarized run settles it. Do not read this key as evidence the question was
      answered.

  WHAT IS NOT HERE, AND WHY. No App Sandbox entitlement: Sonny is not sandboxed, and adding
  com.apple.security.app-sandbox would break local file access across the whole product rather than
  hardening anything. No screen-recording or accessibility entitlement, because neither exists —
  both are TCC grants the user makes in System Settings, not entitlements. No network entitlement,
  because hardened runtime does not restrict outbound network access; that is a sandbox concern.
  Nothing for the other child processes Sonny spawns (/usr/bin/open, /usr/bin/shortcuts): they are
  separate Apple-signed processes carrying their own entitlements, and this app launching them is
  not itself a restricted operation.

  NOT THE RELEASE REQUIREMENT ON ITS OWN. SONNY-106 section E requires a Developer ID signed AND
  notarized build. This file is one prerequisite of the notarization half. It is not evidence that
  the condition is met, and the local development certificate in Packaging/signing-identity does
  nothing for anyone else installing Sonny.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.automation.apple-events</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
